course: biology
lesson: 215
title: Foxes and squirrels
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Two wild animals live right next to us in parks and gardens, and both are cleverer than they look.
explain: A red fox is a member of the dog family, but it hunts alone, climbs fences and pounces like a cat: in winter it can hear a mouse under the snow, leap high and dive nose first right on top of it.
explain: A squirrel is a rodent with teeth that never stop growing, and in autumn it buries thousands of nuts one by one, finding many again by memory and smell.
explain: The ones it forgets grow into new trees, so a squirrel is really a forgetful gardener planting forests, and if another squirrel is watching, it may even pretend to bury a nut to fool the thief.
fact: Foxes scream at night, mostly in winter, to attract mates and mark territory, and people sometimes mistake the sound for a person screaming.
fact: A fox can hear a small animal under snow, leap high and pounce nose first; some scientists think foxes may use the Earth's magnetic field to aim, but this is not yet proven.
fact: Urban foxes live in towns because there is food, shelter and few enemies, but they are wild animals and should not be fed by hand or petted.
fact: In many stories foxes are sly tricksters, because real foxes are clever and adaptable, eat almost anything and learn quickly.
fact: Foxes belong to the dog family, but they hunt alone and climb like cats, and fox cubs are born blind in an underground den.
fact: Squirrels bury thousands of nuts in autumn as food for winter and find many again by memory and smell; the forgotten ones can grow into trees.
fact: A squirrel can run down a tree head first because its back ankles turn round so its claws hook into the bark facing up.
fact: A squirrel's bushy tail helps it balance and works as a blanket and an umbrella.
fact: Studies of grey squirrels found that when another squirrel is watching, a squirrel may pretend to bury a nut to fool the thief, which suggests it plans ahead.
fact: Grey squirrels were brought to Britain from North America; they carry a virus deadly to red squirrels and take their food, so reds now live mostly in Scotland and a few other places such as islands.
fact: Squirrels are skilled acrobats that solve puzzles to reach bird feeders, and some people build squirrel obstacle courses for fun.
fact: Squirrels are rodents whose front teeth grow all the time, about fifteen centimetres a year, and gnawing hard nuts wears them down.
exercise: Why do foxes scream at night in winter? | To attract mates and mark territory | Because they are in pain | Because they are scared of the dark | To call the rain
exercise: Why does a fox dive nose first into the snow? | It has heard a mouse under the snow | It is looking for a warm bed for the night | It is cooling its hot nose in the snow | It is playing a game with other foxes
exercise: Is it proven that foxes aim their pounce using the Earth's magnetic field? | No, some scientists think so but it is not proven | Yes, every fox carries a tiny natural compass inside its ear | Yes, it was proven by the Romans | No, foxes cannot jump at all
exercise: Why do foxes live in towns? | Towns have food, shelter and few enemies | Foxes cannot live anywhere else now the forests are gone | People bring them from forests | They like the street lights
exercise: Why should you not feed a fox by hand or pet it? | It is a wild animal | Foxes eat only grass | Foxes are made of stone | Foxes cannot see hands
exercise: Why are foxes shown as sly in stories? | Real foxes are clever, adaptable and learn quickly | Foxes can talk to people | Foxes steal books | Real foxes are slow and simple, and the stories just make fun of them
exercise: Which family does a fox belong to? | The dog family | The big cat family | The rodent family | The bear family
exercise: How is a fox like a cat? | It hunts alone and climbs fences | It purrs when it is happy and someone strokes it | It has a mane | It eats only fish
exercise: What are fox cubs like when they are born? | Blind, in an underground den | Able to run straight away | Already hunting mice | Born up in a tree nest
exercise: Why does a squirrel bury nuts in autumn? | To store food for winter | To grow its very own nut tree | To hide them from people walking by | Because nuts are too heavy to carry
exercise: How do squirrels help forests grow? | Forgotten buried nuts grow into trees | They water the trees | They plant seeds on purpose with tools | They eat the weeds
exercise: How can a squirrel run down a tree head first? | Its back ankles turn round so its claws hook into the bark | It slides down on its long fluffy tail | It falls and lands on its feet | Its claws are sticky like glue, so it can hold on upside down
exercise: What does a squirrel use its tail for? | Balance, a blanket and an umbrella | Digging holes for nuts in the frozen ground | Cracking nuts | Swimming under water
exercise: Why does a squirrel sometimes pretend to bury a nut? | To fool a squirrel that is watching | Because it has forgotten the nut | To practise digging | To make the farmer laugh
exercise: Where did grey squirrels in Britain come from? | North America | Scotland | Africa | Australia
exercise: How do grey squirrels harm red squirrels? | They spread a deadly virus and take their food | They eat red squirrels | They paint them grey | They steal their tails and their nests and chase them out of every wood
exercise: Why must a squirrel keep gnawing? | Its front teeth grow all the time | Its teeth would fall out if it stopped | Its claws need sharpening on the wood | Gnawing keeps it warm on cold days
exercise: How much do a squirrel's front teeth grow in a year? | About fifteen centimetres | About one millimetre | About one metre | They do not grow at all
test: «heard a woman screaming outside at 2am, mum says it's just a fox» Can that be true? | Yes, foxes scream at night, mostly in winter | No, foxes are silent animals | No, only owls scream like that | Yes, but only in summer, when the cubs are born
test: «2 foxes and 3 squirrels in the park. how many tails» | 5 | 10 | 4 | 3
test: «fox, 2 squirrels n my dog, count the legs. no trunks lol» | 16 | 12 | 14 | 20
test: «a fox is just a cat in disguise lol» What is right? | Foxes are in the dog family, though they hunt and climb a bit like cats | Foxes are just cats with long pointed noses and big bushy tails | Foxes are rodents like squirrels | Foxes are a kind of bear
test: «cute fox in the garden, gonna feed it from my hand» What is right? | No, it is a wild animal and should not be hand fed or petted | Yes, foxes love being stroked and fed, just like a pet dog does | Yes, they only eat from hands | No, foxes only eat grass
test: In Ice Age, Scrat the squirrel spends every film chasing one single acorn. What do real squirrels do? | Bury thousands of nuts and find many again by memory and smell | Keep only one nut for their whole life and guard it from every other animal | Never eat nuts at all | Store nuts only in their cheeks
test: «squirrel buried the nut, dug it up, and buried it again somewhere else, it's daft» Why might it do this? | Another squirrel may be watching, so it fools the thief | It forgot which way is up | It wants the nut to grow into a tree first, so it moves it to the sun | Squirrels are confused by grass
test: What would happen if a squirrel could not gnaw anything hard for months? | Its front teeth would grow too long | Its teeth would fall out | Its tail would get longer | Nothing at all
test: «why are there no red squirrels in my park in London» What is the main reason? | Grey squirrels from America spread a virus to reds and took their food | Red squirrels turn grey in big cities because of all the smoke and dirt | Red squirrels only live in Africa | Red squirrels are scared of pigeons
test: «squirrel's running down the tree head first, how's it not falling» What is right? | Its back ankles turn so its claws hook into the bark | It is falling very slowly | It holds on to the bark with its sharp front teeth all the way | It has suction cups on its feet
test: «squirrel nicked all the bird food AGAIN, bloody thing» Why is this so hard to stop? | Squirrels are skilled acrobats and solve puzzles | Squirrels can fly like birds when they spread their legs out wide | The birds give the food away | Squirrels are invisible at night
test: In Zootopia, Nick Wilde the fox tricks everyone and talks his way out of trouble. Why do stories make foxes sly? | Real foxes are clever, learn quickly and eat almost anything | Real foxes can talk to people | Real foxes are slow and simple | Real foxes always steal from shops and hide the things they take in their dens
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the taxonomic position and habits of the red fox? | It belongs to the dog family, yet hunts alone and climbs in the manner of a cat | It belongs to the cat family and hunts in packs, in the manner of the lion upon the plains | It is a rodent which subsists upon nuts | It is a bear of diminutive size
exam: Identify the principal reason for which grey squirrels endanger the red squirrel in Britain. | Greys transmit a virus fatal to reds and compete for their food | Greys prey upon red squirrels | Greys interbreed with reds and render them grey | Greys occupy only the Scottish islands
exam: It is given that a squirrel is observed feigning the burial of a nut while another squirrel watches. Ascertain the most probable purpose. | To deceive a potential thief | To exercise the muscles of its forepaws | To conceal the nut from the farmer | To cause the nut to germinate
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the incisors, or front teeth, of the squirrel. | They grow continuously and are worn down by gnawing | They cease growing at birth | They are shed and replaced each winter | They are absent in adult squirrels
